## 3. Core Unit Testing Rules (Mandatory Compliance)

| Rule | Principle | Why | Reference |
|---|---|---|---|
| **U1** | **Test behavior, not implementation.** Assert outputs, raised errors, emitted states, and calls at port boundaries; never private methods or internal fields. | Implementation-coupled tests break on safe refactors. | [02-doubles-fixtures-and-quality]({{ref_02}}) |
| **U2** | **Pass/Default + every handled Fail + Empty/Edge.** Cover success, each handled error, and empty/null/boundary inputs per changed behavior. | Happy-path-only suites pass while production fails on edge inputs. | [01-test-matrix-and-structure]({{ref_01}}) |
| **U3** | **Red-to-Green regression test for bug/finding fixes.** Reproduce the failure red first, then fix and run green. | Prevents silent regression of fixed defects. | [01-test-matrix-and-structure]({{ref_01}}) |
| **U4** | **100% Isolated and fast.** Zero live network, database, file system, OS plugins, or wall clock in unit tests. | Live I/O makes unit tests slow, flaky, and environment-dependent. | [02-doubles-fixtures-and-quality]({{ref_02}}) |
| **U5** | **Double the port boundary, never the subject.** Fake or mock the port interface the subject depends on; always test the real subject class/function. | Mocking the subject tests nothing about actual code execution. | [02-doubles-fixtures-and-quality]({{ref_02}}) |
| **U6** | **Never mock authorization, data-scope guards, or the database.** Keep policy/permission checks real; test SQL queries in integration tests. | Mocking security guards away conceals authorization flaws. | [02-doubles-fixtures-and-quality]({{ref_02}}) |
| **U7** | **Deterministic execution.** Inject clocks, IDs, and seeded random generators; await all async calls; isolate state per test. | Flaky tests train developers to ignore red builds. | [02-doubles-fixtures-and-quality]({{ref_02}}) |
| **U8** | **Specific assertions with independent expectations.** Assert exact values, error types/codes, and state sequences from design literals; no tautologies (`isNotNull` alone, `assert True`). | Weak or recomputed assertions pass on wrong output. | [02-doubles-fixtures-and-quality]({{ref_02}}) |
| **U9** | **One behavior per test, AAA structure, behavior-revealing name.** Separate Arrange, Act, Assert; name tests for behavior, condition, and outcome. | Clear failures point straight to the broken rule. | [01-test-matrix-and-structure]({{ref_01}}) |
| **U10** | **Test path mirrors source path.** Place unit tests in the mirrored test directory tree (`├──`/`└──`). | Undiscoverable tests are neither run nor maintained. | [01-test-matrix-and-structure]({{ref_01}}) |
| **U11** | **Reuse workspace setup.** Use the workspace's existing test runner, fixtures, builders, and port fakes (`EXTEND` vs `INIT`). | Competing test setups fragment the suite. | [02-doubles-fixtures-and-quality]({{ref_02}}) |
| **U12** | **Every new test must execute.** Verify collection, non-skipped execution, right-reason red, and green pass. | An unrun or skipped test is `NOT_RUN`, never `PASS`. | [03-execution-boundaries-and-review]({{ref_03}}) |
| **U13** | **State unit test boundaries.** Explicitly report what unit tests proved and what belongs to integration/visual verification. | Over-claiming unit results hides untested integration risk. | [03-execution-boundaries-and-review]({{ref_03}}) |
| **U14** | **Failing test first per the order table.** Run the new test red before writing production code (or set code aside to verify red). | Seeing red proves the test can detect the break. | [03-execution-boundaries-and-review]({{ref_03}}) |

